  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}+2y=20.$ The tangent line at the point $(2,8)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,8).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2y & =20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\ 2x+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,8)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,8)} \\\\  & ={(-x)|}_{(2,\\,8)} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent line at the point $(2,8)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-8 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-2x+12 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(6,\\,0).$",
